Replace-From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Replace-Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 20:55:06 +0800
Subject: watchdog: sp805: service the counter inside the first countdown window

WDTLOAD counts down twice before the reset line asserts: the first
underflow raises an interrupt, the second one is what resets, and this
driver never requests that interrupt line.
With max_hw_heartbeat_ms unset the core sizes its keepalive by the whole
timeout, which for this block is the window up to the unclaimed
interrupt and not the one up to the reset, and the heartbeat worker is
only requested once the driver declares a hardware maximum smaller than
what userspace asked for.
Declare the first countdown as the limit, max_hw_heartbeat_ms = timeout
* 500, so the core feeds at a quarter of the programmed window and keeps
feeding on its own for longer requests.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)


diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/sp805_wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/sp805_wdt.c
index 925f12157181..a9a14c717ba1 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/sp805_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/sp805_wdt.c
@@ -111,6 +111,14 @@ static int wdt_setload(struct watchdog_device *wdd, unsigned int timeout)
 	wdt->load_val = load;
 	/* roundup timeout to closest positive integer value */
 	wdd->timeout = div_u64((load + 1) * 2 + (rate / 2), rate);
+	/*
+	 * WDTLOAD counts down twice: the first underflow raises the interrupt,
+	 * the second one is what resets.  This driver never requests the
+	 * interrupt line, so nothing claims the first underflow -- the
+	 * framework has to service the counter strictly inside the first
+	 * window, not at its edge: its keepalive is max_hw_heartbeat_ms / 2.
+	 */
+	wdd->max_hw_heartbeat_ms = wdd->timeout * 500;
 	spin_unlock(&wdt->lock);
 
 	return 0;
